From ca3e667b61c24eb7f7cbe218466c6072c157b860 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: x <x@example.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 18:21:36 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(state): one deleted-WAL holder scan in flight per
 process; clean cache tracks sidecar identity [daveey]

On macOS each refuse_deleted_wal_generation call ran a libproc pass over every
pid's fds on a fresh daemon thread and abandoned it after 5 s. Under GIL
pressure every ctypes call waits for the GIL, so passes (~50 ms uncontended)
timed out while their threads kept running: a Desktop backend on 2026-10-27
had 4-5 live _scan threads and logged a timeout every few seconds.

883b05f36b added a lock around the pass, but the lock was released when the
caller's wait expired while the thread kept running, so the next caller still
started a second pass beside it. Now a single _DarwinFdScan is shared: callers
that arrive while one is alive wait on it, callers that find it past its
deadline fail open at once, and the pass checks its deadline per descriptor and
exits once past it. It keeps only -wal/-shm vnodes so callers for different
databases can share it. A timeout still fails open.

883b05f36b's 30 s clean-verdict cache also hid a WAL generation retired inside
the window (test_iter_finds_self_after_wal_unlink_on_darwin failed on main).
The cached verdict now holds only while the directory mtime and the -wal/-shm
identities are unchanged; WAL writes touch neither, so busy databases keep it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
